[38;5;231m⏺[39m Here's the design. [1mSection 1: architecture and data model.[0m

  A single new module, [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m, loaded by [38;5;153mindex.html[39m via a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — matching the existing
  no-build, no-module-system pattern ([38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain global script; the CommonJS in [38;5;153msrc/[39m is a separate, unrelated
  half of the repo and stays untouched).

  It exposes a narrow interface over [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m:

  Prefs.get(key, fallback)   // parsed value, or fallback
  Prefs.set(key, value)      // returns true/false; never throws
  Prefs.remove(key)
  Prefs.clear()

  All preferences live in [1mone[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m entry under a single namespaced key ([38;5;153mwebapp.prefs[39m) holding a JSON object,
  rather than one [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m key per preference. One key means adding a preference later needs no new storage
  plumbing, and [38;5;153mclear()[39m can't accidentally wipe unrelated keys the page might use. The cost is that every write
  re-serializes the whole object — irrelevant at this size.

  The [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m calls are confined to [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m, so swapping in a server-backed store later is a change to one file
  rather than to every call site.

  Does that shape look right before I go on to error handling and the UI wiring?

